@arc home.mum
who: home:mum
name: Mum
// The player's people back home (the user's ruling, 2026-10-17: a fixed cast, Mum, Gran, Jo and Sam; home stays
// unnamed, just "home"). ONE-SIDED CALLS (the user, 2026-10-17 evening; head writer 4): one call a day, you choose
// who; only they speak, you listen. No Letters night, no replies. Each call is a little scene, 5-10 lines. The arc's
// six beats come in order; between them, ordinary calls (`== chat`) that match where the arc is (`beat=home.mum.N`).
// The social & traditions engineer plays them (claude/social-fixes).
//
// HOW SHE TALKS: fast, in questions, then answers them herself, then catches herself ("Listen to me."). Calls you
//   {me}, or "pet". Practical worry: food, sleep, socks. Drives the number nine bus; tells you about the route. Laughs
//   at herself first. On the relay she just talks: the delay makes it easier, and she's good at talking.
// NEVER: guilt-trips you for leaving, says "I'm lonely" straight out (she says "the house is quiet"), or genders you.
// GRAN lives with her (Gran is her mum); she shouts things from the other room. JO is your older sibling (they).
// Don't run ahead of Gran's arc (the fall and the hip are Gran's to tell) or Jo's (the boatyard is Jo's to tell).
// RUNNING: the pottery (from beat 2), the number nine, Gran's commentary, the relay delay, your shelf, the kettle.

// ---- the arc: worry to pride; the empty room becomes a pottery room; a lopsided mug in the post bag ----
== beat 1
tone: light
mem: firstcall
flag: home.mum.called
them: Is that you? {me}? There's a delay. Is that the delay? Hello!   [laugh]
them: The lady at the relay office says it's easier if I just talk and you listen. So I'll talk. I'm good at talking.
them: Are you eating? Is there food? Proper food, I mean, not just biscuits.
them: Don't answer that. I can't hear you anyway. I'll just assume vegetables.
them: Gran says hello. She says it louder than that. She's in the kitchen.
them: The number nine misses you. It told me. Well, the driver did. Same thing.
them: Right. I'll let you go. You've got a ship to run. Call again? Call again.

== beat 2
tone: light
when: beat:home.mum.1
mem: pottery
flag: home.mum.pottery
them: Guess what I've done. No, don't guess, it'll take a week with this delay. I've signed up for pottery.
them: Tuesday nights, at the hall. There's eight of us and a man called Rolf who's been doing it forty years.
them: I made a bowl. It isn't a bowl. Gran says it's a hat. Gran's wearing it.   [laugh]
them: I've always wanted to try. For years. I just never went. Then the house went quiet, and I thought, well. Go.
them: Next week, a cup. A proper one. With a handle. Probably.
them: Listen to me, going on about clay. You've got stars. Love to you, pet.

== beat 3
tone: heartfelt
when: beat:home.mum.2
mem: room
flag: home.mum.room
them: Hello, pet. Line's clear today. I can almost hear your kettle.
them: Listen. I've been sitting in your room. Just sitting. The house is quiet without you.   [sigh]
them: I'd like to put a wheel in there. For the pottery. Rolf's selling his old one.
them: Your shelf stays. Every last thing on it. I'll dust round it. Carefully.
them: And I'll keep the window clear. You liked the window. Still your room. Just muddier.
them: Gran says she's having the chair. Gran's always having the chair.
them: There. I've said it. Now I'll go and feel silly about it. Bye, pet.

== beat 4
tone: light
when: beat:home.mum.3
mem: wheel
them: The wheel's in! It spins. Mostly. I've had clay in my hair since Sunday.   [laugh]
them: Rolf brought it round in a wheelbarrow. Down our street. Everyone came out to watch.
them: Gran sits in your chair and tells me I'm doing it wrong. She's never touched clay in her life.
them: And Jo came round and made a little pot. Better than mine. Don't tell them I said.
them: Your shelf's fine. I put a cloth over it. Like a birdcage at night.
them: The bus is late tomorrow, they say. I'll be on the wheel instead. Living dangerously.

== beat 5
tone: serious
when: beat:home.mum.4
mem: nearly
flag: home.mum.told
hint: Mum sounds like she's got something to say.
them: Can I tell you something? I've been carrying it about. Like a pot that's too hot.   [sigh]
them: When you got the letter for the Wren, I nearly asked you not to go. I had the words ready.
them: I'd practised them on the bus. The whole number nine route, there and back.
them: I didn't, because you'd have stayed. For me. And then you'd have been here, wishing.
them: You're out there because you're meant to be. I can hear it in your voice, even with the delay.
them: Don't you dare come home early on my account. There. It's out. I feel lighter.
them: Right. Enough of that. I'm going to make a cup of tea and look at a hat.   [laugh]

== beat 6
tone: resolution
when: beat:home.mum.5
mem: mug
flag: home.mum.mug
keepsake: Mum's mug
learn: Mum made you a mug on her wheel, in the room that used to be yours. It leans. It holds tea.
mail: 2880 | From home | A parcel came up in the post bag: one mug, wrapped in a scarf, a little lopsided. A note inside: "It holds tea. Mostly. Love, Mum."
them: Has it come yet? The parcel? No, it won't have. The post's slow. It's on its way.   [laugh]
them: I made you a mug. On the wheel, in your room. Our room. It leans a bit. Like the bus.
them: Rolf says it's the best thing I've made. Rolf says that about everything. I'm choosing to believe him.
them: It holds tea. Mostly. Every time you drink from it, that's me saying "Are you eating?"
them: Thank you for ringing, every time. The house isn't quiet any more. It's got clay in it. And you, on the line.
them: Off you go. Ship to run. I love you. Gran says she does too. Louder than that.

// ---- ordinary calls, a whole little scene each, separated by --. `when:` keeps them in step with the arc. ----
== chat
// any time
them: Hello! Is it you? It's you. The kettle's on. Not that you can have any.   [laugh]
them: I had the early shift. The number nine at six in the morning. Just me and the milkman and a man with a goose.
them: I don't know where the goose was going. I didn't ask. You don't ask, on the early shift.
them: The goose looked happy enough. Happier than the milkman. He'd lost his hat.
them: Are you sleeping? You sound like you're sleeping. That's a mother's guess.
them: Listen to me. You've got a whole ship and I'm telling you about a goose.
them: Love to you. Gran says love. Jo says nothing, Jo's asleep. Bye, pet.
--
them: {me}! I was just thinking about ringing you. Well, I can't ring you. But I was thinking it.
them: I went to the market. They had those little pears you liked. I bought six and ate four on the bus.
them: The other two are in the bowl. Waiting for you. They won't wait that long. I'll eat them.   [laugh]
them: Socks. Have you got enough socks? It's cold in space, isn't it? It looks cold.
them: Gran wants to know what you're eating. I said stars. She didn't laugh. She never laughs at mine.
them: Listen to me. Pears and socks. You've got proper work to do.
them: Right. Off you go. Sleep. Eat. Don't touch anything that sparks.
--
them: Hello, pet. Quick one, I'm on my break. Sitting in the depot in my coat.
them: The new driver on Sundays takes the corner by the chapel too fast. Gran's written a letter about it.
them: She hasn't sent it. She just reads it out. At dinner. Every night.
them: I've heard it forty times now. It's very good. It says "reckless" four times.
them: Are you warm? Is it warm in there? It's freezing in the depot. They've lost the heater again.
them: Do you have buses up there? Little carts? I can't picture it. I keep picturing a bus with stars outside.
them: Break's over. Number nine waits for no one. Except old Mr Pask. Bye, pet.
--
them: Are you there? You're there. I'm going to pretend you just said "Hello, Mum." Lovely. Hello.
them: I've been cleaning the oven. You don't want to hear about the oven. Nobody does. It's very clean.
them: I could see my face in it. I didn't like my face in it. I've shut the door.   [laugh]
them: Gran made a crumble. It's mostly crumble. There's a pear in there somewhere, she says.
them: I put a bit aside for you. That's silly, isn't it. I'll have it for you.
them: Is there pudding up there? There must be pudding. Listen to me.
them: Love to you, pet. Mind how you go.
--
them: Can you hear rain? It's raining here. Proper rain. I held the receiver up to the window.   [laugh]
them: Did you hear it? You won't have. It's on the delay. You'll get yesterday's rain tomorrow.
them: I thought you might miss it. Do you have weather? You must have something.
them: Mrs Ellery ran out for her washing. She does that every time. She never learns.
them: Gran's put the buckets out in the hall again. The roof's fine. She just likes the buckets.
them: Listen to me, talking about buckets to someone on a starship.
them: There. That's your rain. Go and be dry. Bye, pet.
--
when: time:evening
them: Evening, pet. I've got my feet up. First time today. The bus was full of schoolchildren.
them: One of them asked me if I'd ever been to space. I said no, but my {me} has.
them: Well, I said your name. Whole bus went quiet. Then a boy said "Cool." So you're cool now. Official.
them: Then he asked if you'd met any aliens. I said not yet, but you're very polite, so it'll go fine.
them: Are you tired? You sound tired. Is that the delay? Or is it me? It's me.   [laugh]
them: Get to bed at a proper time. I know it's a different time up there. Get to bed at a proper one anyway.
them: Night night, pet.
--
them: Hello! I've got two minutes and a lot of words. Here they come.
them: Gran's got a cold, she's fine, she's furious about it. The cat from next door got in again. Jo came for tea.
them: The bin men came on the wrong day. Mrs Ellery's hedge has a bird's nest in it. I've bought a new mop.
them: The number nine's got new seats. Blue. Very bouncy. A lady bounced.
them: Did I say Gran's got a cold? I did. She'd want it said twice.
them: That's everything. That's all of home. You're up to date.   [laugh]
them: Love you, pet. Bye.
--
them: You know what I did today? I walked past your old school. Just because.
them: They've painted the gate. Green. It used to be blue. I nearly wrote to the council.
them: Do you remember that gate? You swung on it every morning. I told you off every morning.
them: Then I thought, you don't even go there any more. You go to space. So I let it go.
them: It's still the wrong green, mind.
them: Gran says it's a lovely green. Gran would. She's never once agreed with me about a colour.
them: Bye, pet. Look after yourself.
--
when: weekday:sun
them: Sunday! Sunday roast. Well, Sunday something. Gran's in charge, so it's anyone's guess.
them: I've laid a place for you. Don't laugh. Gran puts the salt on it. It's where the salt lives now.
them: Jo did the potatoes. They were very good. Don't tell them I said.
them: We do say your name at the table, you know. Every Sunday. "Wonder what {me}'s eating."
them: Then Gran says "Not this, lucky thing." And we laugh. Every week. Same joke. It's a good one.
them: Are you having a roast up there? Is there gravy? There must be gravy somewhere in space.
them: Off you go, pet. Have a nice Sunday, whatever day it is up there.
--
them: Can I tell you a small thing? It's very small. A robin sat on the back fence this morning.
them: It sat there all through my breakfast. Looking at me. I said "What?" It didn't say.
them: Was it hungry? Was it lost? Was it judging my toast? I think it was judging my toast.   [laugh]
them: Gran said it wanted my crusts. So I gave it my crusts. Off it went, happy. So was I.
them: That's it. That's the whole story. I just wanted to tell somebody, and you're my somebody.
them: Go on. Back to work. Bye, pet.
--
them: Hello, love. I've had a long day, so I'm going to talk slowly and you can't stop me.   [laugh]
them: A man left his shopping on the bus. Three tins of peaches and a birthday cake. I drove it back to his stop.
them: He was still standing there. Looking lost. When he saw the bus come back round, he cried. Over a cake.
them: He gave me a slice. I ate it at the lights. Don't tell the depot.
them: People are lovely, really. Mostly. Remember that, out there.
them: Did you eat today? Properly? I'm only asking. I always ask.
them: Bye, pet. Eat something nice.
--
them: Is the line all right? You keep going crackly. Or that's me. I've a crackly cold.
them: Gran's making me honey and lemon. Which means she's making it for herself and giving me the cup after.
them: My nose is as red as a post box. The passengers keep giving me sweets. I'm doing very well out of it.
them: Don't worry about me. I'm only telling you so you know I'm a real person with a real nose.   [laugh]
them: Wrap up. Whatever wrapping up is, up there.
them: Have you got a jumper? Socks? Two pairs? I'll stop. I'll stop.
them: Bye, pet. Love you.

// after beat 1: early days, worry and the quiet house
--
when: beat=home.mum.1
them: Hello, pet! Second time's easier. I've got a list. I wrote a list.
them: One: are you eating. I know, I know. Two: are you making friends. Three: is your bed comfy.
them: Four: is anybody being unkind to you. Because I'll come up there. I'll get the bus.
them: Five: socks. That's not a question. Wear them.
them: I can't hear the answers, so I'm going to imagine yes, yes, yes and no. Lovely. Good list.
them: I've put it on the fridge. Gran's added a six. It just says "cake".   [laugh]
them: Bye, pet. Call again.
--
when: beat=home.mum.1
them: I keep setting out two mugs. Then I put one back. Then I get it out again for Gran.
them: She doesn't even want tea. She wants to watch me make it. It's a whole performance, our kitchen.
them: Are you sleeping all right? New bed, new ship. You never slept well the first night anywhere.
them: The house is quiet. Not bad quiet. Just quiet. Like it's listening for you.
them: There. I said I wouldn't say that. Ignore me. Tell your ship I said hello.
them: Gran says tell it hello too. Louder. She's not sure ships can hear.
them: Bye, pet.
--
when: beat=home.mum.1
them: Your room's exactly as you left it. I've not touched it. Well, I've opened the window.
them: And I've dusted. And I sat on the bed for a minute. But apart from that, exactly as you left it.
them: Gran says I'm being soft. Then she went in and sat on the bed too. I saw her.
them: There was a sock under the bed. Just the one. I've put it on your shelf. It's waiting for its friend.
them: Are you wearing the other one? I bet you are. I bet it's lonely up there. Ha.
them: Is your bunk comfy? Have you got a proper pillow? Ask for two. They'll say yes.
them: Right. Off you go. Bye, pet.

// after beat 2: pottery's started
--
when: beat=home.mum.2
them: Pottery report! It's Tuesday, so there's a report.
them: I made a cup. It has a handle. The handle fell off in the kiln. So I made a cup.   [laugh]
them: The kiln's like an oven, only crosser. Rolf talks to it. Nicely. I think it likes him.
them: Rolf says the first year is just making things that fall over. I'm doing very well, then.
them: Gran's started calling it "Mum's mud". She says it like a disease. "How's the mud?"
them: Are you eating, by the way? I'm asking between pots. Listen to me.
them: The mud's lovely, thank you. Bye, pet.
--
when: beat=home.mum.2
them: There's a woman at pottery who makes only frogs. Little clay frogs. Dozens of them.
them: Nobody knows why. I asked. She said "Why not?" And honestly, pet, I had no answer.
them: Rolf says she's the best in the class. Her frogs never fall over. Mine would.
them: I might make a frog. For you. For your bunk. Every bunk needs a frog.
them: Is there room for a frog on a starship? It's very small. It won't eat much.
them: Don't tell Gran. She'll want one. Then she'll want two.
them: Bye, pet. Ribbit. That's a frog joke.   [laugh]
--
when: beat=home.mum.2
them: I've got clay under my nails and I can't get it out. I drove the whole route with grey fingers.
them: A lady asked if I was a sculptor. I said yes. I've never felt so grand on the number nine.
them: I scrubbed and scrubbed. Gran said leave it, it's a badge. So now I'm wearing it.
them: Gran's still wearing the hat. To the shop now. People ask. She says "My daughter's an artist."
them: She's never said that about anything I've done. Took a lopsided bowl.
them: Well. A lopsided hat.   [laugh]
them: Bye, artist's child. Bye, pet.

// after beat 3: the room, waiting for the wheel
--
when: beat=home.mum.3
them: Rolf's measuring the wheel for the doorway. He says it'll fit. He said that about his van.
them: His van's still a bit stuck in his gate. But he says the wheel's different. Smaller. Rounder.   [laugh]
them: I've moved your bed to the other wall. Under the window. It looks bigger. The room, not the bed.
them: The window gets the morning sun now. Good for drying pots, Rolf says. Good for you too, when you're home.
them: Your shelf's untouched. I promised. I've told Gran it's a museum. She's not allowed to sit near it.
them: She sat near it.
them: Bye, pet.
--
when: beat=home.mum.3
them: I keep standing in the doorway of your room. Planning. Where the clay goes. Where the shelf of pots goes.
them: Then I remember it's your room and I feel funny. Then I remember you said... well. You'd say yes. You would.
them: It'll still smell like you. Under the clay. I'll know.
them: The bed stays. For when you're home. You'll sleep among the pots. Like a bowl on a shelf.   [laugh]
them: Gran says I've stood in that doorway so long she's going to charge me rent.
them: Is it silly? It's not silly. It's a bit silly.
them: Listen to me. Go and do something useful. Bye, pet.
--
when: beat=home.mum.3
them: Gran wants to know if your ship has a cat. She says every ship needs a cat. For the mice.
them: I said there won't be mice in space. She said "That's what they want you to think."   [laugh]
them: She's been reading a book about sailors. It's got her going. She calls the kitchen "the galley" now.
them: She's knitted a little sailor's hat. For the cat. The cat we haven't got. It's on the hook in the hall.
them: Ask about the cat, will you. For Gran. She'll want to know.
them: And eat your greens. Do they grow greens up there? I bet they do. I bet they're lovely.
them: Bye, pet.

// after beat 4: the wheel's in
--
when: beat=home.mum.4
them: I'm on the wheel right now. I've got you on the shoulder. Don't mind the whirring.
them: I'm making a jug. It's going well. It's going... oh. No. It's a plate now.   [laugh]
them: Everything I make turns into a plate in the end. Rolf says that's the clay telling me something.
them: Gran came in to watch and got splashed. She says it's the most fun she's had all year.
them: I'll have a cupboard full of plates. We'll have a party when you're home. Plates for everyone.
them: Are you sleeping? I've started spinning in my sleep. I wake up with my hands going round.
them: Bye, pet. Plate.
--
when: beat=home.mum.4
them: Jo came round again. Made another pot. Annoyingly good. They've got the hands for it.
them: They sat in your room with me for an hour. Not talking. Just the wheel going round.
them: I gave Jo soup in a bowl I made. It leaked a bit. They ate it anyway. That's family.
them: That's nice, isn't it. Having someone to be quiet with. I hope you've got someone like that.
them: Gran says Jo's showing off. Gran says that about everyone who's good at things.
them: Are you warm? Are you eating? You know the list. I'll stop.
them: Bye, pet.
--
when: beat=home.mum.4
them: I've sold a bowl! Mrs Ellery bought one. For her keys. She gave me two coins and a cake.
them: She says she'll want a jug next. A jug! I've never once made a jug. Only plates.
them: I'm a professional now. I've told the depot. They made me buy the biscuits.
them: Gran says she's my agent and wants a cut. Of the cake.
them: Gran says not to let it go to my head. Then she told the whole street.
them: It's silly. But I'm so pleased. Is it silly to be pleased about a bowl?
them: Bye, pet. Professional potter, signing off.   [laugh]

// after beat 5: gentler, closer
--
when: beat=home.mum.5
them: Hello, pet. I feel lighter since I told you. Like I put something down.
them: I've been sleeping better. Gran says I've stopped muttering bus stops in my sleep.
them: I didn't know I did that. Apparently I say "Chapel Road" twice a night.
them: You get your talking in your sleep from me, then. Sorry about that.
them: I'm steadier on the wheel too. Rolf asked what had happened. I said nothing. Something.
them: Are you sleeping? All night, properly? I hope so. I hope you sleep like a log.
them: Bye, pet. Chapel Road.   [laugh]
--
when: beat=home.mum.5
them: I made something today and I'm not telling you what it is. It's a secret. It's drying.
them: It's not a frog. It's not a plate. That's all you get.
them: Don't ask. You can't ask, anyway. Ha. That's handy for once.
them: Gran knows. Gran's sworn to secrecy. Gran has never kept a secret in her life.
them: She just walked past and winked at me. Very loudly. She can't wink quietly.   [laugh]
them: Are you eating? There. That's a clue, if you want one.
them: If she rings you, hang up. Can you hang up? Just don't listen. Bye, pet.
--
when: beat=home.mum.5
them: I was on the top deck of the bus at the end of the route. Nobody on. I looked up.
them: It's lovely up there at night. All the lights of town. Then they stop, and it's just sky.
them: You can't see you, of course. But I looked anyway. I do that now. Every night, end of the route.
them: Are you looking out too? Is there a window? I'd like to think we're both at a window.
them: The other drivers think I'm checking the weather. Let them.
them: Listen to me, going soft at the end of the route.
them: Bye, pet. I'll look up tonight.

// after beat 6: settled, proud
--
when: beat=home.mum.6
them: Has the mug arrived? Is it in one piece? Rolf packed it. Rolf packs like he's posting a baby.
them: If it's broken, don't tell me. Just glue it. Then tell me it's lovely.
them: Does it fit your hand? I sized it from memory. Gran helped. Gran was wrong.   [laugh]
them: Gran wants one now. With her name on. I said get in the queue, Gran.
them: I'm making a teapot to go with it. Matching. That'll take another year at least.
them: Listen to me. It's a mug. It's just a mug. Well. It's not just a mug.
them: Bye, pet. Use the mug.
--
when: beat=home.mum.6
them: I've got a little stall now. Saturdays, at the market. Bowls and plates. Mostly plates.
them: I tell everyone my {me} is on a starship. Then they buy a bowl. I don't know why that works.
them: Gran sits beside me in the hat and tells people it's my early work. It sells.
them: Rolf comes by and pretends to be a customer. He's bought the same plate three times.
them: I sold a frog. Just the one. I'm not ready to talk about it.
them: Did you eat today? A proper meal? I'm asking as a business now. It's very serious.
them: Look at us. A business. Who'd have thought. Bye, pet.   [laugh]
--
when: beat=home.mum.6
them: Do you know what I'm proud of? Not the pots. You. Out there. Doing it.
them: I tell people. I tell people who didn't ask. The milkman knows everything about you.
them: He asked how you're getting on. I talked till his milk went warm.   [laugh]
them: I used to worry all the time. I still worry a bit. But mostly now, I'm just proud.
them: That's all. I just wanted you to hear it. Not over the clay, not over Gran. Just: proud.
them: Gran's proud too. She won't say it. She'll shout it from the kitchen in a minute.
them: Right. Off you go, before I start. Bye, pet.

// news: she's heard it in your messages
--
when: flag:intro.soloJob
them: You did a job on your own! Start to finish! Your message came this morning.
them: I read it on the bus at the lights. I read it to the passengers. Some of them clapped.
them: I don't know what a scrubber is. I told them it's very important. Is it? It sounds important.
them: Gran wants to know if they paid you extra. I said it's a ship, Gran, not a bus.
them: Did you have your lunch after? You need your lunch after a big job. Listen to me.
them: On your own. Oh, pet. I'm telling the whole route.
them: Bye. Bye, my clever thing.
--
when: cue:event.ionStorm.afterglow
them: A storm? In space? Is that allowed? Your message said everyone's fine. Are you fine? You're fine.
them: I've been out in the garden looking up. I couldn't see anything. Just Mrs Ellery's washing.
them: Did the lights flicker? Ours flicker when the kettle's on. Maybe it's the same thing.   [laugh]
them: Gran says storms are good luck. She says that about everything bad. Burnt toast. Her knee.
them: Listen to me, telling you about storms. You were in it. I was in the garden.
them: Wear a jumper. I don't know if it helps with space storms. It can't hurt.
them: Bye, pet.
--
when: came:any
them: You went to the {trad}! You wrote and told me. I'm so glad you went.
them: You always said you'd hide at a party. And there you are, going to things. On a starship.
them: Did you talk to anyone? Course you did. Did you eat something? Course you did.
them: Was there music? Did you dance? You won't have danced. You might have tapped a foot.
them: Gran says she went to every party going, at your age. Gran says a lot of things.   [laugh]
them: Next time, tell me what everyone wore. I want details. Gran wants details.
them: Bye, pet.
--
when: friend:any
them: You've got a friend! {friend}. Say it again. {friend}. I'm writing it on the calendar.
them: Gran wants to know if they're nice. I said of course they're nice, they picked {me}.
them: Tell {friend} hello from me. Feed them a biscuit. Is that allowed up there?
them: Do they like tea? Everyone should like tea. If they don't, be kind anyway.
them: Gran says bring them home for Sunday dinner. I said it's a long bus ride, Gran.
them: Oh, pet. A friend. That's all I wanted. Well. That and vegetables.   [laugh]
them: Bye. Love to you both.
